.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="The Butler-Volmer equation writes the net electrode current as the difference of an anodic and a cathodic exponential. At zero overpotential the two partial currents are equal (each is i_0) and cancel -- a dynamic equilibrium; far from equilibrium one term dominates and the equation becomes Tafel&#x27;s straight line. This example plots both partial currents and butler_volmer_current_density, shows the effect of the transfer coefficient \alpha, and checks numerically how fast the Tafel limit is reached.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/butler_volmer/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_butler_volmer_equation_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/butler_volmer/plot_02_butler_volmer_equation`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Butler-Volmer equation: anodic and cathodic partial currents</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/butler_volmer/plot_02_butler_volmer_equation`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Randles and Ševčík (1948) independently showed that the peak current of a reversible linear-sweep voltammogram grows with the square root of the scan rate, i_p = 0.4463\,nFAC\sqrt{nFvD/RT}. This example evaluates randles_sevcik_peak_current over a range of scan rates, and recovers the diffusion coefficient from the slope of the i_p vs. \sqrt{v} plot -- the standard diagnostic for a diffusion-controlled electrode reaction.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/electrochem/voltammetry/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_randles_sevcik_cyclic_voltammetry_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/electrochem/voltammetry/plot_03_randles_sevcik_cyclic_voltammetry`

