.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="For a reaction between perfect crystals, Nernst found that \Delta G and \Delta H approach each other with zero slope as T \to 0, so \Delta S = -\partial\Delta G/\partial T \to 0. With Debye T^3 heat capacities, \Delta C_p = \alpha T^3, the curves are \Delta H = \Delta H_0 + \alpha T^4/4, \Delta S = \alpha T^3/3, and \Delta G = \Delta H_0 - \alpha T^4/12. Planck&#x27;s stronger form, S \to 0 for every perfect crystal, gives absolute entropies from calorimetry, S(T) = \int_0^T C_p/T&#x27;\,dT&#x27;. Those absolute entropies underlie the standard Gibbs energies of formation that gibbs_energy_of_mixture takes as input.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/thermo/thermochemistry/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_nernst_heat_theorem_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/thermochemistry/plot_02_nernst_heat_theorem`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Nernst's heat theorem: reaction entropy vanishes at absolute zero</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/thermo/thermochemistry/plot_02_nernst_heat_theorem`

